position $(R,0)$ when $t=0.$\n\nIn our case, we have $R=2$ and $\\omega =4\\text{ rad/s},$ so we get that the position function is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =2(\\operatorname{cos}⁡4t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t\\,j).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe find the velocity vector by differentiating the position vector. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =(2(\\operatorname{cos}⁡4t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t\\,j))^{′} \\\\  & =-8\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t\\,i+8\\operatorname{cos}⁡4t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we find the velocity vector at $t=\\frac{\\pi }{4},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =-8\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4})\\,i+8\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4})\\,j \\\\  & =-8\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi \\,i+8\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi \\,j \\\\  & =-8\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534214 17592186045417 true] [17592186173162 1064 "Consider the unit circle configured in the usual way in the coordinate plane. Suppose a particle $P$ is initially at the point $(1,0)$ and moves counterclockwise along the unit circle with a fixed **angular velocity** of $\\dfrac\\pi3$ **radians per second.** \n\nThe first three seconds of the particle's motion are illustrated below.\n\n![](images/00/009b83d5d0e48c6983950a48523838cc569b3a92511dc9362630316b1d88f86b.png)\n\n    \n\nSince the angular velocity is constant, we refer to this as **uniform circular motion**. This fixed angular velocity is denoted $\\omega,$ and we write\n\n$$\n\\omega = \\dfrac\\pi 3\\,\\textrm{rad/s}.\n$$\n\nNow, let $\\mathbf r(t)$ be the position vector of $P$ at time $t.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\mathbf {r}(t)= \\cos\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi t}{3}\\right) \\, \\mathbf i + \\sin\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi t}{3}\\right) \\,\\mathbf j.\n$$\n\nIt's worth building a picture of why this formula is true:\n\n- We know that $\\mathbf r(\\theta) = \\cos\\theta\\,\\mathbf i + \\sin\\theta\\,\\mathbf j$ traces out a unit circle, starting at the point $(1,0).$\n\n- We can see from the picture that $P$ will return to the starting position after $t=6$ seconds, so the angular frequency of the motion is\n\n$$\n\\omega = \\dfrac{2\\pi}{6} = \\dfrac{\\pi}{3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\cdot t)\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\cdot t)\\,j \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi t}{3})\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi t}{3})\\,j\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $P$ indeed returns to its starting location when $t=6{:}$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(6) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{6\\pi }{3})\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{6\\pi }{3})\\,j \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\pi )\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\pi )\\,j \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)\\,j \\\\  & =r(0).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn general, if a particle moves counterclockwise along a circle of radius $R$ centered at $O$ with initial position $(R,0),$ then its position vector $\\mathbf r(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf {r}(t)= R\\left(\\cos\\omega t \\, \\mathbf i + \\sin\\omega t \\,\\mathbf j\\right)\\!\\,.\n$$\n\nNote that for clockwise motion, we have $\\omega < 0.$\n\nLet's see an example." 13194139534214 17592186045417 true] [17592186173162 1064 "Consider the unit circle configured in the usual way in the coordinate plane. Suppose a particle $P$ is initially at the point $(1,0)$ and moves counterclockwise along the unit circle with a fixed **angular velocity** of $\\dfrac\\pi3$ **radians per second.** \n\nThe first three seconds of the particle's motion are illustrated below.\n\n![](images/00/009b83d5d0e48c6983950a48523838cc569b3a92511dc9362630316b1d88f86b.png)\n\nSince the angular velocity is constant, we refer to this as **uniform circular motion**. This fixed angular velocity is denoted $\\omega,$ and we write\n\n$$\n\\omega = \\dfrac\\pi 3\\,\\textrm{rad/s}.\n$$\n\nNow, let $\\mathbf r(t)$ be the position vector of $P$ at time $t.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\mathbf {r}(t)= \\cos\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi t}{3}\\right) \\, \\mathbf i + \\sin\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi t}{3}\\right) \\,\\mathbf j.\n$$\n\nIt's worth building a picture of why this formula is true:\n\n- We know that $\\mathbf r(\\theta) = \\cos\\theta\\,\\mathbf i + \\sin\\theta\\,\\mathbf j$ traces out a unit circle, starting at the point $(1,0).$\n\n- We can see from the picture that $P$ will return to the starting position after $t=6$ seconds, so the angular frequency of the motion is\n\n$$\n\\omega = \\dfrac{2\\pi}{6} = \\dfrac{\\pi}{3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =\\operatorname{cos}(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\cdot t)\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\cdot t)\\,j \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}(\\frac{\\pi t}{3})\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{\\pi t}{3})\\,j\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $P$ indeed returns to its starting location when $t=6{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(6) & =\\operatorname{cos}(\\frac{6\\pi }{3})\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{6\\pi }{3})\\,j \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}(2\\pi )\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}(2\\pi )\\,j \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}(0)\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}(0)\\,j \\\\  & =r(0).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn general, if a particle moves counterclockwise along a circle of radius $R$ centered at $O$ with initial position $(R,0),$ then its position vector $\\mathbf r(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf {r}(t)= R\\left(\\cos\\omega t \\, \\mathbf i + \\sin\\omega t \\,\\mathbf j\\right)\\!\\,.\n$$\n\nNote that for clockwise motion, we have $\\omega < 0.$\n\nLet's see an example." 13194139534214 17592186045417 false] [17592186173205 1064 "Suppose a particle $P$ moves along a circle of radius $R$ centered at the origin in the $xy$-plane. \n\n![](images/45/4564f4c4fcd8068f3bf1645a310afab7fcdc2f82fec6966cb220558c1e93639a.png)\n\n    \n\nNote the following:\n\n- The **position vector** $\\mathbf r$ extends from the origin to $P$ in the *radial* direction, while\n\n- the **velocity vector** $\\mathbf v$ points in the direction of the particle's motion, *tangential* to the circle.\n\nFirst, let's assume that $P$ is at the point $(R,0)$ when $t=0.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t) = R\\left(\\cos\\omega t \\, \\mathbf i + \\sin\\omega t \\,\\mathbf j\\right).\n$$\n\nWe can easily derive a convenient formula relating $\\mathbf v$ and $\\omega$ by differentiating $\\mathbf r(t){:}$\n\nDifferentiating once, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & ={r}^{′}(t) \\\\  & ={(R(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t\\,j))}^{′} \\\\  & =R\\omega (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t\\,j).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy computing the magnitude of this vector, we see that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}||v|{|}^{2} & ={R}^{2}{\\omega }^{2}((-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t)^{2}+{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t)}^{2}) \\\\  & ={R}^{2}{\\omega }^{2}({sin}^{2}⁡\\omega t+{cos}^{2}⁡\\omega t) \\\\  & ={R}^{2}{\\omega }^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTaking the square root, we get\n\n$$\n||\\mathbf v || = R|\\omega|.\n$$\n\nNote that while the magnitude of the velocity vector (i.e., the speed) is constant, its direction is continuously changing, which means that an object in uniform circular motion also experiences *acceleration* (more on this shortly)." 13194139534214 17592186045417 true] [17592186173205 1064 "Suppose a particle $P$ moves along a circle of radius $R$ centered at the origin in the $xy$-plane. \n\n![](images/45/4564f4c4fcd8068f3bf1645a310afab7fcdc2f82fec6966cb220558c1e93639a.png)\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The **position vector** $\\mathbf r$ extends from the origin to $P$ in the *radial* direction, while\n\n- the **velocity vector** $\\mathbf v$ points in the direction of the particle's motion, *tangential* to the circle.\n\nFirst, let's assume that $P$ is at the point $(R,0)$ when $t=0.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t) = R\\left(\\cos\\omega t \\, \\mathbf i + \\sin\\omega t \\,\\mathbf j\\right).\n$$\n\nWe can easily derive a convenient formula relating $\\mathbf v$ and $\\omega$ by differentiating $\\mathbf r(t){:}$\n\nDifferentiating once, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & ={r}^{'}(t) \\\\  & ={(R(\\operatorname{cos}\\omega t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}\\omega t\\,j))}^{'} \\\\  & =R\\omega (-\\operatorname{sin}\\omega t\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}\\omega t\\,j).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy computing the magnitude of this vector, we see that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}||v|{|}^{2} & ={R}^{2}{\\omega }^{2}((-\\operatorname{sin}\\omega t)^{2}+{(\\operatorname{cos}\\omega t)}^{2}) \\\\  & ={R}^{2}{\\omega }^{2}({sin}^{2}\\omega t+{cos}^{2}\\omega t) \\\\  & ={R}^{2}{\\omega }^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTaking the square root, we get\n\n$$\n||\\mathbf v || = R|\\omega|.\n$$\n\nNote that while the magnitude of the velocity vector (i.e., the speed) is constant, its direction is continuously changing, which means that an object in uniform circular motion also experiences *acceleration* (more on this shortly)." 13194139534214 17592186045417 false] [17592186173260 1064 "Let's return to our particle $P$ moving along a circle of radius $R$ centered at the origin in the $xy$ plane. \n\n![](images/45/4564f4c4fcd8068f3bf1645a310afab7fcdc2f82fec6966cb220558c1e93639a.png)\n\n    \n\nOnce again, let's assume that $P$ is at the point $(R,0)$ when $t=0.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t) = R\\left(\\cos\\omega t \\, \\mathbf i + \\sin\\omega t \\,\\mathbf j\\right).\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we get the velocity vector $\\mathbf v{:}$ \n\n$$\n\\mathbf v(t) =R\\omega\\left(-\\sin\\omega t \\, \\mathbf i + \\cos\\omega t \\,\\mathbf j\\right)\n$$\n\nDifferentiating once more, we get an expression for the acceleration vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & ={v}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =R\\omega {(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t\\,j)}^{′} \\\\  & =R\\omega (-\\omega \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t\\,i-\\omega \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t\\,j) \\\\  & =-{\\omega }^{2}\\cdot R(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t\\,j) \\\\  & =-{\\omega }^{2}r\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo summarize,\n\n$$\n\\mathbf a = -\\omega^2 \\mathbf r.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the acceleration vector acts in the *opposite* direction to $\\mathbf r.$ In other words, $\\mathbf a$ points *toward the center of the circle*. Moreover, we have\n\n$$\n||\\mathbf a|| = \\omega^2 || \\mathbf r ||.\n$$" 13194139534214 17592186045417 true] [17592186173260 1064 "Let's return to our particle $P$ moving along a circle of radius $R$ centered at the origin in the $xy$ plane. \n\n![](images/45/4564f4c4fcd8068f3bf1645a310afab7fcdc2f82fec6966cb220558c1e93639a.png)\n\nOnce again, let's assume that $P$ is at the point $(R,0)$ when $t=0.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t) = R\\left(\\cos\\omega t \\, \\mathbf i + \\sin\\omega t \\,\\mathbf j\\right).\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we get the velocity vector $\\mathbf v{:}$ \n\n$$\n\\mathbf v(t) =R\\omega\\left(-\\sin\\omega t \\, \\mathbf i + \\cos\\omega t \\,\\mathbf j\\right)\n$$\n\nDifferentiating once more, we get an expression for the acceleration vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & ={v}^{'}(t) \\\\  & =R\\omega {(-\\operatorname{sin}\\omega t\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}\\omega t\\,j)}^{'} \\\\  & =R\\omega (-\\omega \\operatorname{cos}\\omega t\\,i-\\omega \\operatorname{sin}\\omega t\\,j) \\\\  & =-{\\omega }^{2}\\cdot R(\\operatorname{cos}\\omega t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}\\omega t\\,j) \\\\  & =-{\\omega }^{2}r\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo summarize,\n\n$$\n\\mathbf a = -\\omega^2 \\mathbf r.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the acceleration vector acts in the *opposite* direction to $\\mathbf r.$ In other words, $\\mathbf a$ points *toward the center of the circle*. Moreover, we have\n\n$$\n||\\mathbf a|| = \\omega^2 || \\mathbf r ||.\n$$" 13194139534214 17592186045417 false] [17592186414187 1080 "10\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}" 13194139534214 17592186045417 true] [17592186414187 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139534214 17592186045417 true] [17592186414187 1083 #uuid "0c31bcf8-56dc-552c-82e1-a8f67c126734" 13194139534214 1759218