e] [17592186414222 1075 #edb/ref 17592186414207 13194139534214 17592186045417 true] [17592186414222 1075 #edb/ref 17592186414209 13194139534214 17592186045417 true] [17592186414222 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139534214 17592186045417 true] [17592186414222 1077 "selection" 13194139534214 17592186045417 true] [17592186414222 1078 #edb/ref 17592186414204 13194139534214 17592186045417 true] [17592186414222 1079 #uuid "eb6d2d2c-d5c5-59c6-8a10-7880e9a9bc99" 13194139534214 17592186045417 true] [17592186414223 1068 #edb/ref 17592186414219 13194139534214 17592186045417 true] [17592186414223 1069 "q-97669" 13194139534214 17592186045417 true] [17592186414223 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534214 17592186045417 true] [17592186414223 1071 "An object moves along a circular path with position $r(t)=2(\\operatorname{cos}⁡5t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡5t\\,j)$ meters. Find the magnitude of the acceleration of the object at time $t,$ measured in seconds." 13194139534214 17592186045417 true] [17592186414223 1072 #uuid "6774c327-b1ba-5269-8425-5f0d3e5c0aaa" 13194139534214 17592186045417 true] [17592186414223 1074 "**Method 1**\n\nFirst, we find the velocity vector by differentiating the position vector. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =(r(t))^{′} \\\\  & =(2(\\operatorname{cos}⁡5t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡5t\\,j))^{′} \\\\  & =-10\\operatorname{sin}⁡5t\\,i+10\\operatorname{cos}⁡5t\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we find the acceleration vector by differentiating the velocity vector. \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & =(v(t))^{′} \\\\  & =(-10\\operatorname{sin}⁡5t\\,i+10\\operatorname{cos}⁡5t\\,j)^{′} \\\\  & =-50\\operatorname{cos}⁡5t\\,i-50\\operatorname{sin}⁡5t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the magnitude of the acceleration as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}||a(t)|| & =\\sqrt{(-50\\operatorname{cos}⁡5t)^{2}+(-50\\operatorname{sin}⁡5t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{50}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡5t+{50}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡5t} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{50}^{2}} \\\\  & =50\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the magnitude of the acceleration is $50\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}.$\n\nNote that $||a||$ is constant, but $a$ is not. \n\n**Method 2**\n\nFor uniform circular motion in the $xy$-plane, we have\n\n$$\na=-{\\omega }^{2}r\\,⇒\\,||a||={\\omega }^{2}||r||.\n$$\n\nIn our case, the radius of the circle is $a=2\\,\\text{m}$ and the angular frequency $\\omega =5\\,\\text{rad/s}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n||a||={5}^{2}\\cdot 2=50\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}.\n$$" 13194139534214 17592186045417 true] [17592186414224 1068 #edb/ref 17592186414221 13194139534214 17592186045417 true] [17592186414224 1069 "q-97641" 13194139534214 17592186045417 true] [17592186414224 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534214 17592186045417 true] [17592186414224 1071 "An object moves along a circular path with position $r(t)=\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡4t\\,i+\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t\\,j$ meters. Find the speed of the object at time $t,$ measured in seconds." 13194139534214 17592186045417 true] [17592186414224 1072 #uuid "a62921c3-0ad7-5f20-8061-cad8dd0b028d" 13194139534214 17592186045417 true] [17592186414224 1074 "**Method 1**\n\nFirst, we find the velocity vector by differentiating the position vector. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =(r(t))^{′} \\\\  & =(\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡4t\\,i+\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t)^{′} \\\\  & =-4\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t\\,i+4\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡4t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the speed, we calculate the magnitude of the velocity vector, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}||v(t)|| & =\\sqrt{(-4\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t)^{2}+(4\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡4t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{48{sin}^{2}⁡4t+48{cos}^{2}⁡4t} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{48({sin}^{2}⁡4t+{cos}^{2}⁡4t)} \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the speed of the object is $4\\sqrt{3}\\text{ m/s}.$\n\nNote that the speed is constant, but the velocity is not. \n\n**Method 2**\n\nFor uniform circular motion in the $xy$-plane, we have\n\n$$\n||v||=R|\\omega |,\n$$\n\nwhere $R$ is the radius of the circle, and $\\omega $ is the angular velocity.\n\nIn our case, the radius of the circle is $R=\\sqrt{3}\\,\\text{m}$ and the angular frequency $\\omega =4\\,\\text{rad/s}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n||v||=\\sqrt{3}\\cdot |4|=4\\sqrt{3}\\text{ m/s}.\n$$" 13194139534214 17592186045417 true] [17592186414225 1068 #edb/ref 17592186414218 13194139534214 17592186045417 true] [17592186414225 1069 "q-97667" 13194139534214 17592186045417 true] [17592186414225 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534214 17592186045417 true] [17592186414225 1071 "An object moves along a circular path with position $r(t)=2(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j)$ meters. Find the magnitude of the acceleration of the object at time $t,$ measured in seconds." 13194139534214 17592186045417 true] [17592186414225 1072 #uuid "a71f6e18-3665-5500-82e7-6236632d7d47" 13194139534214 17592186045417 true] [17592186414225 1074 "**Method 1**\n\nFirst, we find the velocity vector by differentiating the position vector. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =(r(t))^{′} \\\\  & =(2(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j))^{′} \\\\  & =-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we find the acceleration vector by differentiating the velocity vector. \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & =(v(t))^{′} \\\\  & =(-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j)^{′} \\\\  & =-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the magnitude of the acceleration as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}||a(t)|| & =\\sqrt{(-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}+(-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4{cos}^{2}⁡t+4{sin}^{2}⁡t} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4} \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the magnitude of the acceleration is $2\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}.$\n\nNote that $||a||$ is constant, but $a$ is not. \n\n**Method 2**\n\nFor uniform circular motion in the $xy$-plane, we have\n\n$$\na=-{\\omega }^{2}r\\,⇒\\,||a||={\\omega }^{2}||r||.\n$$\n\nIn our case, the radius of the circle is $a=2\\,\\text{m}$ and the angular frequency $\\omega =1\\,\\text{rad/s}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n||a||={1}^{2}\\cdot 2=2\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}.\n$$" 13194139534214 17592186045417 true] [17592186414226 1068 #edb/ref 17592186414222 13194139534214 17592186045417 true] [17592186414226 1069 "q-97636" 13194139534214 17592186045417 true] [17592186414226 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534214 17592186045417 true] [17592186414226 1071 "A particle moves along a **clockwise** circular path with center $O$ in the $xy$-plane with a constant angular velocity of $4\\text{ rad/s}.$ Given that the object is at the position $(9,0)$ when $t=0,$ find the position function of the particle." 13194139534214 17592186045417 true] [17592186414226 1072 #uuid "dc0a5ea1-b4e4-5d2f-b036-40e69f17a373" 13194139534214 17592186045417 true] [17592186414226 1074 "The position function of a particle moving in a circular path of radius $R$ in the $xy$-plane with a constant angular velocity $\\omega $ is given by\n\n$$\nr(t)=R(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t\\,j),\n$$\n\nwhere it is assumed that the circle is centered at $O,$ and particle is at the position $(R,0)$ when $t=0.$\n\nSince the object's initial position is $(9,0),$ we have that $r(0)=\\,9i,$ and we can conclude that the radius of the circle $R=9.$\n\nTherefore, since we have $R=9$ and $\\omega =-4\\text{ rad/s}$ (negative because the motion is clockwise), we get that the position function is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =9(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-4t)\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-4t)\\,j) \\\\  & =9(\\operatorname{cos}⁡4t\\,i-\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t\\,j).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534214 17592186045417 true] [17592186414227 1068 #edb/ref 17592186414220 13194139534214 17592186045417 true] [17592186414227 1069 "q-97634" 13194139534214 17592186045417 true] [17592186414227 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534214 17592186045417 true] [17592186414227 1071 "A particle moves along a circular path centered at $O$ in the $xy$-plane in the counterclockwise direction with a constant angular velocity of $\\frac{1}{4}\\text{ rad/s}.$ Given that the object is at the position $(1,0)$ when $t=0,$ find the position function of the particle." 13194139534214 17592186045417 true] [17592186414227 1072 #uuid "f8f962dd-237f-5b45-bf8e-0b4aa69b05bf" 13194139534214 17592186045417 true] [17592186414227 1074 "The position function of a particle moving in a circular path of radius $R$ in the $xy$-plane with a constant angular velocity $\\omega $ is given by\n\n$$\nr(t)=R(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t\\,j),\n$$\n\nwhere it is assumed that the circle is centered at $O,$ and the particle is at the position $(R,0)$ when $t=0.$\n\nSince the object's initial position is $(1,0),$ we have that $r(0)=i,$ and we can conclude that the circle's radius is $R=1.$\n\nTherefore, since we have $R=1$ and $\\omega =\\frac{1}{4}\\text{ rad/s},$ we get that the position function is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\frac{t}{4}\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\frac{t}{4}\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534214 17592186045417 true]] :edb/tempids {"answer-0c31bcf8-56dc-552c-82e1-a8f67c126734" 17592186414187 "answer-126ae55a-4a80-5260-86e9-62365c375f5e" 17592186414188 "answer-1f79c33c-3cd0-568c-bb05-a2c622afa98b" 17592186414189 "answer-31222f3b-2ab4-5b9e-a782-7337c19d97f8" 17592186414190 "answer-3e7e470c-cb69-5444-a607-ccc44143b6f8" 17592186414191 "answer-4c30bb07-72a4-5565-8468-407beb723740" 17592186414192 "answer-4eb2dfc5-f3e1-5fac-9360-84971d459fb6" 17592186414193 "answer-506e4e03-9474-5a31-8c4a-f1ce551d5609" 17592186414194 "answer-69ab6c9f-a3a5-5ce4-8790-f06da39f2e5d" 17592186414195 "answer-6d9f2696-3096-581e-be2a-958e487b42b1" 17592186414196 "answer-7099fb71-cfb9-5667-9c1d-abef4c2a8aa6" 17592186414197 "answer-74489a4f-0f2e-5d3d-b8ce-522969cbe4d4" 17592186414198 "answer-7e5b72a4-dae1-5b76-